\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

Our experiments test three parts of the argument developed above.
We first isolate the failure from Section~\ref{sec:failure} and ask whether standard FB loses the action difference at a control bottleneck.
We then test whether preserving this difference improves manipulation control, first on OGBench and then in MetaWorld and ManiSkill.
Finally, we ask whether the resulting representation retains the main benefit of successor measures: using the same frozen model for rewards specified only after pretraining.

\paragraph{Experimental protocol.}
Unless stated otherwise, all methods use state observations and are trained for $10^6$ gradient steps on the same fixed offline dataset.
We report results over five independent training seeds.
For matched comparisons, we keep the network architecture, task sampling, actor, training budget, and evaluation procedure fixed, and change only the successor-learning objective.
For offline policy learning, we use the flow-based behavior-cloning actor from \citet{bagatella-iclr26a}, based on Flow Q-Learning~\citep{park-icml25a}.
Success rates are averaged over the final three evaluation passes at $0.8$, $0.9$, and $1.0$ million steps, with ten episodes per task.
Full implementation details are given in \aditya{Add Appendix}.

\subsection{Preserving the bottleneck decision}
\label{sec:exp-didactic}

\aditya{Add more envs?}
The failure analysis makes a concrete prediction: a low-rank squared-loss successor model can fit most of the successor measure while losing the smaller difference that determines which action is preferred.
If \name{} addresses this failure, it should preserve this action difference at lower rank than standard FB.
We test this on the nine-state process from Section~\ref{sec:failure}, where the exact successor measure and action values are known.
Both critics use the same fixed uniform continuation policy, transitions, and exact expectations, with no learned actor.

At rank $3$, the exact successor measure assigns values $2.20$ and $0.80$ to the two actions at state $7$.
FB predicts $1.04$ and $1.01$, making the actions nearly indistinguishable, while \name{} predicts $2.00$ and $0.62$.
Across ranks, FB preserves only $1\%$--$2\%$ of the exact action gap at ranks $2$ and $3$ and requires rank $6$ out of nine states to recover $99\%$.
\name{} already preserves $55\%$ at rank $2$ and at least $99\%$ from rank $3$ onward.
Normalizing the backward features to unit norm does not remove the failure.

Thus, FB can capture the dominant successor structure while losing the difference needed for the correct action, whereas \name{} preserves that difference with substantially lower rank.
We next ask whether this difference is large enough to matter for manipulation control.

