\subsection{Manipulation control across benchmarks}
\label{sec:exp-manipulation}

If the bottleneck failure identified above contributes to manipulation performance, then improving the successor estimate should improve control on tasks that require precise interactions with objects.
We test this first on OGBench, where FB, \name{}, HILP~\citep{park-iclr24a}, and RLDP~\citep{bagatella-iclr26a} use the same offline data and evaluation protocol.

The top block of Table~\ref{tab:manipulation} shows that \name{} improves over FB from $45.6$ to $81.3$ on \texttt{cube-single}, from $2.2$ to $30.4$ on \texttt{cube-double}, and from $10.2$ to $52.7$ on \texttt{scene}.
It also exceeds HILP and RLDP on these three domains.
All successor-based methods remain near floor on \texttt{puzzle-3x3}, indicating that changing the successor objective alone does not solve every source of difficulty in manipulation.

We next ask whether these gains are specific to OGBench.
The lower blocks of Table~\ref{tab:manipulation} evaluate the same FB and \name{} comparison on independently collected MetaWorld and ManiSkill datasets, which differ in their dynamics, state spaces, and control interfaces.
\name{} improves over FB on all eight tasks, with particularly large gains on bin picking, cube picking, and peg lifting.

The improvement therefore extends beyond the OGBench cube environments and appears across several manipulation systems.

